                Where This Market Is Heading
              </h2>
              <p style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #09090b; margin-bottom: 18px;">
                A few directional trends worth watching as you make this decision, because the landscape a year from now will look meaningfully different from today's snapshot. OpenTelemetry-native architecture is rapidly becoming table stakes rather than a differentiator: nearly every credible alternative now treats OTel as a first-class citizen, which is steadily lowering the switching cost between platforms and, not coincidentally, putting more competitive pressure on the incumbents' pricing. SQL-based querying is displacing proprietary query languages across a growing share of the market, reducing the learning-curve tax that is used to lock teams into whichever vendor's DSL they'd already learned. And the "AI analyst that investigates" category (platforms that go beyond surfacing an anomaly to actually explaining it) is still young relative to the rest of this list, but it's the fastest-growing differentiator in the space, precisely because it addresses the one part of the job that cheaper dashboards alone were never going to fix.
              </p>
